        "eyebrow": "WHY MIRELLIS EXISTS",
        "heading": "Modern life rarely gives us clear moments of transition.",
        "body": "<p>The same devices that wake us in the morning often remain present until sleep.</p><p>Work follows us home. Messages continue arriving. Information remains available at every hour.</p><p>Even when the day appears to end, attention often remains engaged.</p><p>Over time, many people describe feeling physically tired while remaining mentally engaged—as though one part of the day never fully gives way to the next.</p><p>As we began studying everyday routines, a recurring pattern emerged: many of the natural boundaries that once separated the day had become increasingly blurred.</p><p>Not because people lacked information.</p><p>Not because they lacked productivity tools.</p><p>But because moments of orientation, slowing, reflection and recovery were becoming less distinct in everyday life.</p><p><strong>From that observation grew the question that continues to guide Mirellis: what happens when the transitions between human states begin to disappear?</strong></p>"
